{
  "requirement": "For node reads, the SDK constructs a `Snapshot` from\nthe contents returned by `Domain::node_content` and their\n`NodeContents::fingerprint` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`, without another read.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Snapshot<T> {\n    pub content: T,\n    pub fingerprint: Fingerprint,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Snapshot<T> {\n    pub fn new(content: T, fingerprint: Fingerprint) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<T: Clone> Clone for Snapshot<T>;\nimpl<T: Debug> Debug for Snapshot<T>;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from `requirements.sdk.graph.snapshot`**\n1. For node reads, the SDK builds a `Snapshot` from the contents `Domain::node_content` returns, together with their `NodeContents::fingerprint`.\n2. The SDK builds it without another read.\n3. `Snapshot<T>` has public `content` and `fingerprint` fields, and `Snapshot::new(content, fingerprint)` pairs them.\n4. `Snapshot<T>` is `#[non_exhaustive]` and has `Clone` and `Debug` implementations.\n\n**Test mapping**\n- **Construction from the read (1):**\n  - `snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions` in `validation_tests.rs` calls `read_snapshot` and asserts `.fingerprint == fp(\"before\")`. After the node changes, it asserts that earlier dependencies keep the old fingerprint and that a new read gets the new one.\n  - `direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots` checks `snapshot.content.format()` and `snapshot.fingerprint == fp(original)` for empty and large multi-byte-character content. It also checks that the snapshot is unchanged after the underlying node is replaced.\n- **No additional read (2):**\n  - `identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot` in `tests/contracts.rs` takes a snapshot, mutates the node, and asserts three things. `snapshot.content.fingerprint() == snapshot.fingerprint`. `snapshot.content.format()` is still \"before\". The recorded reads are exactly `[1]`.\n  - `snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions` asserts the exact read log `[1, 1, 1, 2]`. That log is consistent with one read per `read` or `read_snapshot` call.\n  - The agent tests `graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery` and `explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots` cover the agent path. They assert the recorded `Dependency::Node` fingerprints equal the content fingerprint. The latter asserts the read order `[1, 3, 2, 3, 1]`.\n  - `requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk` in `src/sdk.rs` shows that a retained content's fingerprint stays fixed while a fresh read differs after the source changes.\n- **Public shape and `new` (3):** `callers_can_pair_content_with_its_fingerprint` in `tests/contracts.rs` runs from an external crate. It builds a snapshot through `Snapshot::new` and asserts both public fields.\n- **Attributes and trait impls (4):**\n  - I found no test that clones or debug-formats a `Snapshot`.\n  - In `graph.rs#struct[Snapshot]` these are `#[derive(Clone, Debug)]` and `#[non_exhaustive]`. They are declarative and have no conditional logic or transformation, so they fall under the \"no conditional, no transformation\" exemption.\n  - The external-crate test uses `Snapshot::new` rather than a struct literal. That is consistent with `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n\n**Conclusion:** The behavioral obligations have tests that assert their outcomes. The only unexercised parts are compile-time derives and an attribute. No gap needs acknowledgment, and the `{cite}` of `node_content.fingerprint` is applied in the snapshot tests (fingerprint equality with `content.fingerprint()`).",
  "references": "6783b2a4d0b4e2c98352e3255d1bb07342bc13f8749e2cee76ad5948eb461b69",
